Proof. This comes from the short exact sequence of double complexes
$$\begin{CD}
\big( {{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A\big)^G@>>> 
{{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A@>>> 0 \\
@VVV @V\varphi-1 VV@VVV \\
0@>>> {{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A @>>> 
\big({{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A\big)_G
\end{CD}$$
and ${{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(X)\otimes A\cong \big({{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A\big)^G$.
Functoriality and the localization property are well-known
for cycle complexes, and this carries over to 
$\big( {{\mathbb Z}}^c(n)(\bar X)\otimes A\big)_G$ by an easy diagram chase.
\hfill $Q.E.D.$ \\


For a finitely generated field $K$ of transcendence degree $d$ over ${{\mathbb F}}_q$, 
define $H^i_F(K,A(j))$ to be $\operatornamewithlimits{colim} H_{2d+1-i}^F(U,A(d-j))$,
where $U$ runs through smooth schemes with function field $K$.
By \eqref{yyy}, the cohomology groups of $K$ lie in short exact sequences
\begin{multline}\label{frobdescent}
0\to H^{s-t-1}_{\mathcal M}(K\otimes_{{{\mathbb F}}_q}\bar {{\mathbb F}}_q,A(s-n))_G
\to H^{s-t}_F(K,A(s-n))\to \\ 
H^{s-t}_{\mathcal M}(K\otimes_{{{\mathbb F}}_q}\bar {{\mathbb F}}_q,A(s-n))^G\to  0, 
\end{multline}
where $K\otimes_{{{\mathbb F}}_q}\bar {{\mathbb F}}_q$ is a finite product of fields.



